<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liang Shidu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/628">628</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Liang Shidu was a local officer in Shuofang, on the northern bend of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, who killed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> governor in 617 and proclaimed himself emperor of Liang. As a client of the Eastern Turks he held out against the Tang longer than any other regime born of the Sui collapse. He was killed by his cousin in 628 when Tang forces besieged his capital.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Liang Shidu came from a prominent clan of Xia Province, roughly modern Yulin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>, and served as a military officer under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang of Sui</a>. In or shortly before 617 he left the army and returned to his home commandery, by then called Shuofang, where agrarian rebellions were spreading, and gathered men into a local protective force. After ambushing Tang Shizong, an official of the commandery, he first took the title of Grand Chancellor, imitating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-sui">Emperor Wen of Sui</a>, who had acted as regent before taking the throne.</p><p>He allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-turkic-khaganate">Eastern Turkic Khaganate</a>, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> general Zhang Shilong and took the nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> of Diaoyin, Honghua and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yanan">Yan'an</a>. Shibi Qaghan named him Dadupiqie Qaghan and gave him a flag with a wolf head, the symbol of the Turks, while Liang declared a state of Liang with himself as emperor and the era name Yonglong.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozu-of-tang">Li Yuan</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> in 618 as Emperor Gaozu, Liang attacked Ling Prefecture, which had submitted to Tang, in 618 and 619, and Yan Prefecture in 619 and 620, and was repelled each time, often by the Tang general Duan Decao. A Turkic plan to strike Tang from several sides, which Liang had urged on Chuluo Qaghan, lapsed when the qaghan died. In spring 622 Duan took the eastern city of Liang's capital Shuofang and withdrew only when Turkic relief arrived.</p><p>Liang's generals Zhang Ju, Liu Min, He Sui and Suo Tong went over to Tang, and his former ally Guo Zihe turned against him. When the Qihu chieftain Liu Yaocheng came to submit, Liang annexed his forces instead, which made his own officers fearful and distrustful of him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 628, with the Eastern Turks in internal turmoil, Emperor Taizong urged Liang to surrender and, when he refused, sent raiders to burn his crops and agents to bribe and divide his officials. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illig-qaghan">Illig Qaghan</a> offered to trade Liang for Khitan tribes that had gone over to Tang, but Taizong refused. Chai Shao and Xue Wanjun then led a major campaign, the Turkic reinforcements were crushed, and when food ran out Liang Luoren killed his cousin and surrendered the city. The Chinese histories place the death on 28 May or 3 June 628. Tang rewarded Liang Luoren with the title Duke of Shuofang County.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Liang Shidu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
